Short answer: No, not without significant changes. The VPN is a hard blocker.

Here's why:

Browser side — fine ✅
Each main.py runs as a separate Python process with its own memory. The global _browser_instance / _page_instance singletons don't leak between processes, so two separate Chrome windows would work independently.

VPN side — hard blocker ❌

WireGuard on Windows creates a kernel-level virtual network adapter — it's a machine-wide resource, not a per-process one:

One tunnel = one exit IP for the entire machine. Whichever instance last called connect_to_vpn sets the active server. The other instance's browser traffic automatically routes through the same server whether it wants to or not.

Config file race condition. Both instances write to the same mullvad_keys.json and the same WireGuard\Configurations\ directory simultaneously — they'll corrupt each other's configs.

Mullvad account key limit. Each connection registers a WireGuard keypair against your account. Two simultaneous connections need two registered keys, and the current code manages a single key pool.

What would be needed to make it work:

Problem	Solution
Single OS network stack	Run each instance in a separate VM or Docker container (with --network isolation), or use a SOCKS5 proxy per instance instead of a system-wide tunnel
Config file conflicts	Give each instance its own WireGuard interface name (e.g., MullvadVPN_1, MullvadVPN_2) and separate config dirs
Key management	Register 2 keys simultaneously against the Mullvad account
The cleanest approach for true parallel operation is separate VMs or Docker containers — each gets its own network stack and runs main.py normally. On Windows this means WSL2 instances or Hyper-V VMs.